Recoil [
    Title: "Brom DSP exponential ADSR envelope"
    Description: {
        One ADSR, with its curve as a parameter.

        An earlier version of this file claimed Mach's Rust core "reaches the
        same algorithm through the brom-dsp-envelope crate". It does not, and
        that was never checked: the two differ in the exponential constant
        (-100 dB against -60 dB per nominal segment time), in where each stage
        snaps to its endpoint, in whether release has a minimum time, and in
        whether a near-zero segment is instant.

        Every one of those is a voicing choice rather than a different
        algorithm, so they live in adsr-config! beside the times they shape.
        Use adsr-config-brom or adsr-config-mach rather than filling the struct
        by hand -- a wrong curve constant does not look like a bug, it just
        sounds like a different instrument.

        Coefficients are cached in the envelope and recomputed only when the
        parameter behind them moves. Mach runs about fifteen of these per voice
        across sixteen voices, so an exp per envelope per sample would be 240
        transcendentals a sample; the reference does none.

        adsr-tick-n advances several samples at once, which is what lets a host
        stagger envelope updates across voices instead of doing all of them
        every sample.

        Stages are an fsm! rather than a mutable stage integer, so an illegal
        transition is a compile-time question rather than a runtime one.
    }
    Type: module
    Module: brom/dsp/envelope
    Imports: []
    Exports: [
        adsr-config! adsr-envelope!
        ADSR_STAGE_IDLE ADSR_STAGE_ATTACK ADSR_STAGE_DECAY ADSR_STAGE_SUSTAIN ADSR_STAGE_RELEASE
        ADSR_ENDPOINT_LOG ADSR_MACH_ENDPOINT_LOG
        adsr-config-brom adsr-config-mach
        adsr-reset adsr-trigger adsr-release adsr-tick adsr-tick-n
        adsr-active? adsr-level adsr-releasing?
    ]
]

math-ffi: foreign <math.h> [
    exp: "exp" [f64! return: [f64!]]
]

ADSR_STAGE_IDLE: 0
ADSR_STAGE_ATTACK: 1
ADSR_STAGE_DECAY: 2
ADSR_STAGE_SUSTAIN: 3
ADSR_STAGE_RELEASE: 4
ADSR_ENDPOINT_TOLERANCE: 0.00001
; -ln(1e-5): a segment reaches a hundred decibels below its span in its nominal
; time. This is the curve Loos has always had.
ADSR_ENDPOINT_LOG: 11.512925464970229
; Sixty decibels in the same nominal time, so every segment sounds faster and
; more abrupt than the constant above. Mach's curve.
;
; 6.908 and not -ln(1e-3) = 6.907755..., which is what the name suggests and
; what this said first. The reference is written with the rounded literal, and
; rounding it back to the exact value moved the curve by 3.5e-5 relative --
; small per sample, but it is in the exponent, so a couple of hundred samples of
; attack compound it to 1e-5 of level and every A/B case failed. The constant is
; whatever the instrument uses, not whatever the mathematics says it should be.
ADSR_MACH_ENDPOINT_LOG: 6.908
; Sentinel for "no coefficient computed yet". No real segment time is negative,
; so the first tick after a reset always recomputes whatever it is asked for.
ADSR_CACHE_UNSET: -1.0

adsr-machine!: make fsm! [
    events: [
        trigger ["Hard retrigger from zero"]
        attack-complete ["Move from attack to decay"]
        decay-complete ["Move from decay to sustain"]
        release ["Start the release segment"]
        release-complete ["Become idle after release"]
        reset ["Return immediately to idle"]
    ]
    idle [
        trigger => attack
        reset => idle
    ]
    attack [
        trigger => attack
        attack-complete => decay
        release => release
        reset => idle
    ]
    decay [
        trigger => attack
        decay-complete => sustain
        release => release
        reset => idle
    ]
    sustain [
        trigger => attack
        release => release
        reset => idle
    ]
    release [
        trigger => attack
        release-complete => idle
        reset => idle
    ]
]

adsr-config!: make c-struct! [
    attack: [f64!]
    decay: [f64!]
    sustain: [f64!]
    release: [f64!]
    ; --- curve, not timing ---------------------------------------------------
    ; How steeply a segment approaches its target: larger is a sharper knee.
    endpoint-log: [f64!]
    ; How close to one the attack gets before it is called finished. Reaching a
    ; target exponentially takes forever, so every implementation of this
    ; decides somewhere to stop, and where it stops is audible on short times.
    attack-snap: [f64!]
    ; The same decision for decay and release, which approach from above.
    level-snap: [f64!]
    ; Release times shorter than this are treated as this. Guards against a
    ; click when a note is cut, at the cost of the shortest releases.
    release-floor: [f64!]
    ; A segment shorter than this is instant rather than very fast.
    min-time: [f64!]
]

adsr-envelope!: make c-struct! [
    stage: [i32!]
    level: [f64!]
    ; The per-segment coefficients, and the parameters they were derived from.
    ; Held here rather than in the config because the config is the caller's and
    ; may be shared across every voice, while the cache is per envelope.
    attack-coefficient: [f64!]
    decay-coefficient: [f64!]
    release-coefficient: [f64!]
    cached-attack: [f64!]
    cached-decay: [f64!]
    cached-release: [f64!]
]

adsr-config-brom: func [
    "Build an ADSR configuration on Brom's own curve, the one Loos uses."
    attack [f64!] "Attack time in seconds."
    decay [f64!] "Decay time in seconds."
    sustain [f64!] "Sustain level, zero to one."
    release [f64!] "Release time in seconds."
    return: [adsr-config!] "Configuration reaching a hundred decibels down per nominal time."
] [
    return make adsr-config! [
        attack: attack
        decay: decay
        sustain: sustain
        release: release
        endpoint-log: ADSR_ENDPOINT_LOG
        attack-snap: ADSR_ENDPOINT_TOLERANCE
        level-snap: ADSR_ENDPOINT_TOLERANCE
        release-floor: 0.0
        min-time: 0.0
    ]
]

adsr-config-mach: func [
    "Build an ADSR configuration on Mach's curve."
    attack [f64!] "Attack time in seconds."
    decay [f64!] "Decay time in seconds."
    sustain [f64!] "Sustain level, zero to one."
    release [f64!] "Release time in seconds."
    return: [adsr-config!] "Configuration reaching sixty decibels down per nominal time."
] [
    return make adsr-config! [
        attack: attack
        decay: decay
        sustain: sustain
        release: release
        endpoint-log: ADSR_MACH_ENDPOINT_LOG
        ; Attack is called done at 0.999, decay and release at a ten-thousandth.
        attack-snap: 0.001
        level-snap: 0.0001
        release-floor: 0.005
        min-time: 0.0001
    ]
]

adsr-stage-number: func [
    "Return the public numeric stage constant for an ADSR state machine."
    machine [adsr-machine!] "ADSR state machine whose current state is inspected."
    return: [i32!] "One of the ADSR_STAGE_* constants."
] [
    if state? machine adsr-machine!/attack [return ADSR_STAGE_ATTACK]
    if state? machine adsr-machine!/decay [return ADSR_STAGE_DECAY]
    if state? machine adsr-machine!/sustain [return ADSR_STAGE_SUSTAIN]
    if state? machine adsr-machine!/release [return ADSR_STAGE_RELEASE]
    return ADSR_STAGE_IDLE
]

adsr-machine-from-stage: func [
    "Reconstruct the ADSR state machine represented by a public stage constant."
    stage [i32!] "Public ADSR_STAGE_* value stored in an envelope."
    return: [adsr-machine!] "FSM value in the matching state, or idle for an invalid stage."
] [
    if stage = ADSR_STAGE_ATTACK [return adsr-machine!/attack]
    if stage = ADSR_STAGE_DECAY [return adsr-machine!/decay]
    if stage = ADSR_STAGE_SUSTAIN [return adsr-machine!/sustain]
    if stage = ADSR_STAGE_RELEASE [return adsr-machine!/release]
    return adsr-machine!/idle
]

adsr-clamp-sustain: func [
    "Clamp an ADSR sustain target to the normalized envelope range."
    value [f64!] "Requested sustain level."
    return: [f64!] "Normalized sustain level in the inclusive zero-to-one range."
] [
    if value < 0.0 [return 0.0]
    if value > 1.0 [return 1.0]
    return value
]

adsr-reset: func [
    "Create an idle ADSR envelope at exactly zero level."
    return: [adsr-envelope!] "Fresh inactive envelope value with no cached coefficients."
] [
    return make adsr-envelope! [
        stage: ADSR_STAGE_IDLE
        level: 0.0
        attack-coefficient: 0.0
        decay-coefficient: 0.0
        release-coefficient: 0.0
        cached-attack: ADSR_CACHE_UNSET
        cached-decay: ADSR_CACHE_UNSET
        cached-release: ADSR_CACHE_UNSET
    ]
]

adsr-trigger: func [
    "Hard-retrigger an ADSR envelope from zero into its attack stage."
    envelope [adsr-envelope!] "Previous envelope value, replaced by the returned value."
    return: [adsr-envelope!] "Active zero-level envelope in the attack stage."
] [
    ; The cached coefficients survive the retrigger. They describe the
    ; configuration, not the note, and a synth retriggering sixteen voices on a
    ; chord would otherwise recompute every one of them for no reason.
    machine: make [#mutable adsr-machine!] adsr-machine!/idle
    send machine trigger []
    out: make [#mutable adsr-envelope!] envelope
    out/stage: adsr-stage-number machine
    out/level: 0.0
    return out
]

adsr-release: func [
    "Start the release stage while preserving the envelope's current level."
    envelope [adsr-envelope!] "Current envelope value whose level starts the release."
    return: [adsr-envelope!] "Envelope value in the release stage."
] [
    machine: make [#mutable adsr-machine!] adsr-machine-from-stage envelope/stage
    if state? machine adsr-machine!/idle [return adsr-reset]
    send machine release []
    out: make [#mutable adsr-envelope!] envelope
    out/stage: adsr-stage-number machine
    return out
]

adsr-releasing?: func [
    "Report whether an ADSR envelope is in its release stage."
    envelope [adsr-envelope!] "Envelope value to inspect."
    return: [logic!] "True only during release."
] [
    return envelope/stage = ADSR_STAGE_RELEASE
]

adsr-active?: func [
    "Report whether an ADSR envelope has not reached its idle stage."
    envelope [adsr-envelope!] "Envelope value to inspect."
    return: [logic!] "True when the envelope is in attack, decay, sustain, or release."
] [
    machine: adsr-machine-from-stage envelope/stage
    if state? machine adsr-machine!/idle [return false]
    return true
]

adsr-level: func [
    "Return the current normalized envelope level."
    envelope [adsr-envelope!] "Envelope value to inspect."
    return: [f64!] "Current level, exactly zero for a reset or completed release."
] [
    return envelope/level
]

adsr-coefficient: func [
    "Per-sample multiplier that carries a segment toward its target."
    seconds [f64!] "Segment duration in seconds; zero or less yields an instant segment."
    sample-rate [f64!] "Processing sample rate in samples per second."
    endpoint-log [f64!] "Curve constant: how far down the segment reaches in its nominal time."
    return: [f64!] "Multiplier in zero to one; smaller converges faster."
] [
    ; A segment of no length has no rate. adsr-advance-segment already treats
    ; one as instant and never reads this, but the cache refresh computes every
    ; coefficient whether its segment is used or not -- so the degenerate case
    ; has to answer here rather than divide.
    samples: seconds * sample-rate
    if samples <= 0.0 [return 0.0]
    return math-ffi/exp (0.0 - (endpoint-log / samples))
]

adsr-coefficient-pow: func [
    "Raise a segment coefficient to a whole number of samples."
    coefficient [f64!] "Per-sample multiplier."
    steps [i32!] "Number of samples to advance; one or more."
    return: [f64!] "The multiplier for advancing that many samples at once."
] [
    ; Repeated multiplication rather than exp(steps * ln coefficient): steps is
    ; a handful, so this is both cheaper and exactly equal to taking the same
    ; number of single steps, which is what makes a strided update indis-
    ; tinguishable from an unstrided one.
    out: make [#mutable f64!] 1.0
    repeat i steps [out: out * coefficient]
    return out
]

adsr-advance-segment: func [
    "Advance one exponential segment by a number of samples, snapping at its endpoint."
    envelope [adsr-envelope!] "Envelope value at the start of this segment."
    target [f64!] "Normalized level approached by this segment."
    seconds [f64!] "Configured segment duration in seconds."
    coefficient [f64!] "Cached per-sample multiplier for this segment."
    steps [i32!] "Number of samples to advance."
    snap [f64!] "Distance from the target at which the segment is called finished."
    min-time [f64!] "Segment durations at or below this are instant."
    return: [adsr-envelope!] "Updated envelope, its level snapped if it reached the endpoint band."
] [
    out: make [#mutable adsr-envelope!] envelope
    if seconds <= min-time [
        out/level: target
        return out
    ]
    distance: target - out/level
    out/level: target - (distance * (adsr-coefficient-pow coefficient steps))
    if (out/level - target) < snap [
        if (target - out/level) < snap [
            out/level: target
        ]
    ]
    return out
]

adsr-refresh-cache: func [
    "Recompute whichever segment coefficients their parameters have invalidated."
    envelope [adsr-envelope!] "Envelope whose cache is being brought up to date."
    config [adsr-config!] "Current configuration."
    sample-rate [f64!] "Processing sample rate in samples per second."
    return: [adsr-envelope!] "Envelope with coefficients matching the configuration."
] [
    out: make [#mutable adsr-envelope!] envelope
    if out/cached-attack <> config/attack [
        out/cached-attack: config/attack
        out/attack-coefficient: adsr-coefficient config/attack sample-rate config/endpoint-log
    ]
    if out/cached-decay <> config/decay [
        out/cached-decay: config/decay
        out/decay-coefficient: adsr-coefficient config/decay sample-rate config/endpoint-log
    ]
    if out/cached-release <> config/release [
        out/cached-release: config/release
        floored: either config/release < config/release-floor [config/release-floor] [config/release]
        out/release-coefficient: adsr-coefficient floored sample-rate config/endpoint-log
    ]
    return out
]

adsr-tick-n: func [
    "Advance an ADSR envelope by several samples at once."
    envelope [adsr-envelope!] "Envelope value at the current sample."
    config [adsr-config!] "Segment times, sustain level and curve."
    steps [i32!] "Number of samples to advance; one or more."
    sample-rate [f64!] "Processing sample rate in samples per second."
    return: [adsr-envelope!] "Envelope advanced by that many samples."
] [
    ; Advancing n samples at once is exactly n single steps, because within a
    ; segment the envelope is a geometric sequence. What it cannot do is notice
    ; a stage boundary partway through the group -- the envelope leaves attack
    ; up to n-1 samples late. That is the trade a strided update makes, and it
    ; is why the step count belongs to the caller.
    if sample-rate <= 0.0 [return envelope]
    out: make [#mutable adsr-envelope!] adsr-refresh-cache envelope config sample-rate
    machine: make [#mutable adsr-machine!] adsr-machine-from-stage out/stage
    if state? machine adsr-machine!/idle [
        out/level: 0.0
        return out
    ]
    if state? machine adsr-machine!/attack [
        out: adsr-advance-segment out 1.0 config/attack out/attack-coefficient
            steps config/attack-snap config/min-time
        if out/level = 1.0 [send machine attack-complete []]
        out/stage: adsr-stage-number machine
        return out
    ]
    if state? machine adsr-machine!/decay [
        sustain: adsr-clamp-sustain config/sustain
        out: adsr-advance-segment out sustain config/decay out/decay-coefficient
            steps config/level-snap config/min-time
        if out/level = sustain [send machine decay-complete []]
        out/stage: adsr-stage-number machine
        return out
    ]
    if state? machine adsr-machine!/sustain [
        out/level: adsr-clamp-sustain config/sustain
        out/stage: adsr-stage-number machine
        return out
    ]
    if state? machine adsr-machine!/release [
        out: adsr-advance-segment out 0.0 config/release out/release-coefficient
            steps config/level-snap config/min-time
        if out/level = 0.0 [send machine release-complete []]
        out/stage: adsr-stage-number machine
        return out
    ]
    return adsr-reset
]

adsr-tick: func [
    "Advance an ADSR envelope by one sample."
    envelope [adsr-envelope!] "Envelope value at the current sample."
    config [adsr-config!] "Segment times, sustain level and curve."
    sample-rate [f64!] "Processing sample rate in samples per second."
    return: [adsr-envelope!] "Next envelope value; invalid sample rates leave it unchanged."
] [
    return adsr-tick-n envelope config 1 sample-rate
]
